<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Athens</h1><p class="st-meta">1205 CE – 1458 CE · capital Thebes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Crusader state of Athens and Thebes ruled in turn by Burgundian, Catalan and Florentine lords for two and a half centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1205 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> granted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> to the Burgundian knight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho-de-la-roche">Otho de la Roche</a>, who took the title Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I</a> was styled Duke of Athens by the mid-13th century. The duchy covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, and was nominally subject to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> family the duchy prospered, with its court at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> church that turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> into a Catholic cathedral of Our Lady. Greek peasants remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. In 1311 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> killed Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-v-of-brienne">Walter of Brienne</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros">Halmyros</a> and ruled the duchy for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio I Acciaioli</a>, a Florentine banker's son, took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> in 1388 and founded a new ducal line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who already exacted tribute, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1456 and the Acropolis in 1458, annexing the duchy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-athens.jpg" alt="Duchy of Athens" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_in_1210.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1205 CE – 1458 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thebes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otho de la Roche, Walter of Brienne, Nerio I Acciaioli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Catalan, Italian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lordship of Athens, Duchy of Athens and Thebes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1332&amp;focus=duchy-of-athens" aria-label="Duchy of Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5260 801 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Athens lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5580 1057L5563 982M5580 1057L5530 1071M5580 1057L5333 1064M5580 1057L4984 945M5580 1057L5709 970M5580 1057L5554 1046M5580 1057L5556 1051M5580 1057L5570 1082"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5563 982h0M5530 1071h0M5333 1064h0M4984 945h0M5